     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-10-16 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>All Weather Portfolio Shows Lower Volatility Than Equity Alternatives</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#finance</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A Zerodha Varsity backtest comparing investment strategies from 2011 to 2026 found that Ray Dalio’s All Weather portfolio returned 9.9% annually, trailing the 10.4% return of a 60:40 portfolio and the 12% Nifty 500 TRI return. However, the All Weather strategy demonstrated significantly lower volatility at 5.7% and a smaller peak-to-trough decline of 6.4%.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-16/all-weather-portfolio-shows-lower-volatility-than-equity-alt.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>During the Covid-19 market crash, the All Weather portfolio fell 6.4%, compared to 17.6% for the 60:40 portfolio and 31.8% for the Nifty 500 TRI. While this strategy offers resilience through gold and bond diversification, its performance remains subject to how various asset classes respond to specific economic conditions.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise and accurate reporting of the backtest results and the risk-reward trade-offs.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/ray-dalio-s-all-weather-portfolio-vs-60-40-portfolio-which-delivered-better-returns-over-15-years-in-india-11792150453117.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
