     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-10-16 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Chicago Temple Attack Plot Uncovered: Accused Vowed to 'Kill Indians'</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Chicago</span> <span>#Temple Attack</span> <span>#Hate Crime</span> <span>#Arrest</span></div>
       <p>A 19-year-old man, Alexandros Paporakis, has been accused of plotting a mass shooting at the Sri Venkateswara Swami (Balaji) Temple in Aurora, Illinois, located about 40 miles west of downtown Chicago. Prosecutors allege Paporakis told co-workers at a Taco Bell outlet that he “couldn’t wait to kill Indian people” and that Sunday would be the “special day,” prompting them to alert the police. He was arrested on October 11 following these reports.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-16/chicago-temple-attack-plot-uncovered-accused-vowed-to-kill-i.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Paporakis faces felony charges for making a terrorist threat and unlawfully possessing a rifle. According to prosecutors, he informed a co-worker on Sunday afternoon that his gun had jammed and he had left the temple's parking lot, vowing to “retry” the attack. Palatine Police Chief William Nord confirmed a red Acura RDX registered to Paporakis entered the temple’s parking lot at approximately 12:25 pm on Sunday, departing less than 15 minutes later. Investigators also believe Paporakis sent the co-worker a video showing an AR-style rifle and a photograph of himself wearing a tactical vest.</p><p>Authorities found an eight-page document titled “Why I Fought: A.K.A. (Aurora Shooter’s Manifesto)” that Paporakis allegedly sent to his co-worker. This document reportedly contained racist language targeting non-white people, outlining a plan to kill as many as possible, describing them as “subhumans” and expressing a desire to “sacrifice myself to take revenge on the world.” Paporakis reportedly identified himself as a “sociopathic white supremacist Neo Nazi.” During a search of his residence, detectives recovered an AR-style rifle without a serial number, multiple loaded rifle magazines, hundreds of rounds of ammunition, a tactical vest with body armour, a patch depicting Nazi symbolism, and a gas canister labelled with a slur referring to South Asians. Paporakis’ mother was questioned but is not cooperating with the investigation.</p><p>In response to the threat, the Sri Venkateswara Swami (Balaji) Temple has implemented additional security measures. Illinois Representative Raja Krishnamoorthi, who visited the temple on Sunday unaware of the threat, has increased his own security and urged the FBI to investigate and prosecute the matter as a hate crime, also calling for federal nonprofit security grants for religious institutions. Aurora Mayor John Laesch is contacting other area temples to inform them of the threat. India’s Ministry of External Affairs expressed “deep concern” over the incident’s “anti-Hindu and racist nature,” expecting US authorities to investigate thoroughly and prevent future occurrences. The incident occurred as the temple was celebrating Navratri.</p><p>Paporakis appeared in court on Wednesday and was ordered to remain in detention, with his next court hearing scheduled for October 30. The Cook County Public Defender’s office, representing Paporakis, cautioned that statements by police and prosecutors are allegations and cases can change significantly during investigation.</p><p>What to watch: The ongoing investigation and Paporakis's next court hearing on October 30 will be key developments.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report is accurate and comprehensive, effectively incorporating the details from both the summary and the full text provided.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/world/chicago-temple-attack-accused-alexandros-paporakis-said-couldnt-wait-to-kill-indian-people-illinois-hindu-temple-ws-l-10384256.html" rel="noopener">News18 World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
