     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-10-16 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India, Japan Discuss Advanced Road Infrastructure Cooperation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#India</span> <span>#Japan</span> <span>#Infrastructure</span> <span>#Nitin Gadkari</span></div>
       <p>India and Japan have engaged in comprehensive discussions aimed at bolstering cooperation in road infrastructure, with a particular emphasis on advanced construction technologies, road safety, and sustainable development. Union Road Transport and Highways Minister Nitin Gadkari addressed the 12th session of the India-Japan Joint Working Group (JWG) on Roads and Road Transportation Sector on Thursday.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-16/india-japan-discuss-advanced-road-infrastructure-cooperation.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>During the session, Minister Gadkari underscored the critical need for developing modern, safe, and sustainable roads. These infrastructure improvements are intended to support economic growth while simultaneously enhancing convenience and the quality of life for citizens. The meeting served as a crucial platform for both nations to exchange technical expertise and identify opportunities for collaborative efforts in the development and management of road infrastructure.</p><p>The discussions delved into various key areas, including advanced construction methods, specialized tunnel and bridge engineering, and the broader scope of sustainable infrastructure development. The integration of digital technologies in road management was also a focal point, facilitating a comprehensive exchange of technical knowledge, experience, and best practices between Indian and Japanese experts. Both countries explored avenues to strengthen cooperation across the entire lifecycle of road infrastructure, from planning and construction to operation and maintenance.</p><p>Experts from technical institutions and industry representatives from both nations presented their insights and engaged in interactive question-and-answer sessions. The deliberations highlighted the importance of adopting innovative technologies and sustainable practices to improve road infrastructure quality, efficiency, and management. This collaboration aligns with India's Viksit Bharat @2047 vision, which aims to transform the nation into a developed country by its independence centenary, anticipating that enhanced road connectivity will drive economic growth, logistics efficiency, and expand regional opportunities.</p><p>What to watch: Implementation of advanced road construction technologies.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Fully mirrors the joint working‑group summary; no extraneous or unverified statements.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/transportation/roadways/nitin-gadkari-looks-to-japan-for-better-roads-in-india-from-advanced-construction-technology-to-safer-tunnels-and-bridges-heres-what-was-discussed/articleshow/134999254.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
