     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-10-16 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Indian Family Offices Boost Homegrown AI Firms Amid Global Race</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#AI</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Investments</span> <span>#Family Offices</span></div>
       <p>Indian billionaire family offices and wealthy investors are increasingly backing homegrown AI startups, seeking to gain ground in the global artificial intelligence race. This push comes as India's private investment in its AI economy totaled over $15 billion between 2013 and 2025, a figure that trails significantly behind the US, which saw $757 billion, and China, with nearly $132 billion in its AI ecosystem during the same period. At least six homegrown AI companies have already achieved valuations exceeding $1 billion, signaling the emergence of a new generation of technology firms.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-16/indian-family-offices-boost-homegrown-ai-firms-amid-global-r.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Among the prominent investors leading this charge are billionaire tech founder Azim Premji’s family office, WhatsApp Inc.’s Chief Executive Officer Kunal Shah’s QED Innovation Labs, and Manipal Group Chairman Ranjan Pai’s family office. Riddhiman Jain, head of investments at Waterfield Advisors, noted that many family offices and angel investors are now keen on direct investments as they become more comfortable with India’s evolving AI ecosystem, emphasizing the need for patient capital in this early-stage investing universe. Premji Invest, recognized as the largest Indian family office investing globally in AI, recently participated in a $27 million funding round for AI verification platform Pramaana Labs in June. Ranjan Pai’s Claypond Capital has also increased its investments this year, including a $40 million round in content startup Flam in September and a $130 million round in vibe coding platform Emergent in July.</p><p>This influx of capital is fostering a new wave of firms specializing in AI models, enterprise software, coding, and various applications. Anirudh Damani, managing partner at Artha Venture Fund, described the situation as “the start of a major upswing on AI investments in India,” highlighting India’s cost advantage in providing software to global companies and developing AI applications. Notable successes include Mumbai-based Fractal Analytics Ltd., which raised $313 million in an initial public offering in February, becoming India’s first AI unicorn to go public. Uniphore, building AI models and applications, was valued at $2.5 billion last year after a $260 million late-stage funding round backed by Nvidia Corp. and Advanced Micro Devices Inc. Additionally, Sarvam, founded in 2023, achieved a $1.5 billion valuation this year after raising $345 million, supported by investors like HCL Technologies Ltd., and is developing AI models in 22 Indian languages.</p><p>Despite the growing private sector interest, a World Bank Group report indicates India faces constraints in capital, computing infrastructure, and specialized research talent. While the US private sector drives AI investments and Beijing plans to spend 2 trillion yuan ($300 billion) on data centers over five years, the Indian government’s ₹10,370 crore ($1.1 billion) IndiaAI Mission focuses on public infrastructure like computing capacity and sovereign AI models. Major Indian tycoons Gautam Adani and Mukesh Ambani have committed approximately $210 billion in data centers, semiconductors, and AI-related services. The Tata Group is also investing about $12.5 billion in two semiconductor plants and developing an $11 billion AI Innovation City near Mumbai, while Google, Amazon, and Microsoft have announced around $45 billion in data-center investments in India. Damani believes India's rapid adoption of new technologies will enable it to quickly catch up in AI applications and adoption.</p><p>What to watch: The continued growth of India's AI ecosystem, driven by both private investment and government initiatives, will be a key area of development.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a high-density summary of the provided source text, capturing the key investors and the state of the Indian AI investment landscape.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/companies/news/india-s-family-offices-bet-on-homegrown-ai-firms-to-catch-up-with-us-china-126101600176_1.html" rel="noopener">Business Standard</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
