     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-16 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>NASA's Hubble Space Telescope Captures Edge-On Spiral Galaxy NGC 1827</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The NASA Hubble Space Telescope has captured an image of the edge-on, intermediate spiral galaxy NGC 1827, located roughly 37 million light-years away in the constellation Columba, the Dove. The image reveals bright-blue regions and dark dust lanes along the galaxy's disk where new stars are likely forming.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-16/nasas-hubble-space-telescope-captures-edge-on-spiral-galaxy-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This observation is part of a larger survey targeting 260 satellite galaxies around 22 Milky Way-like host galaxies. Hubble has already observed 111 of these targets, with the remaining 149 galaxies requiring an additional 149 orbits to complete the survey.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately captures key survey details and statistics directly from the full text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-nasa-hubble-spies-spiral-edge.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
