     <article class="story single" data-beat="entertainment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENTERTAINMENT</span> · TOI Entertainment · 2026-10-16 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Reese Witherspoon Reclaims Full Ownership of Hello Sunshine</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Reese Witherspoon</span> <span>#Hello Sunshine</span> <span>#Candle Media</span> <span>#Pierre-Antoine Capton</span></div>
       <p>Reese Witherspoon has successfully reclaimed full ownership of her media agency, Hello Sunshine, which she originally started in 2016. This significant move was facilitated through a recent agreement where Pierre-Antoine Capton, co-founder and CEO of a European independent studio giant, partnered with Witherspoon to buy back the production company from Blackstone-backed Candle Media. Hello Sunshine had been acquired by Candle Media in 2021 for USD 900 million, a transaction that occurred during the height of the streaming war spending boom.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-16/reese-witherspoon-reclaims-full-ownership-of-hello-sunshine.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>According to Variety, Capton’s company and Witherspoon have now bought back Hello Sunshine for a discount, reportedly in the low-to-mid eight figures. This deal places the space back under Witherspoon’s direct control. Capton confirmed the specifics of the transaction, stating, “The bottom line is that we acquired 100% of Hello Sunshine along with Reese and Mediawan, from Candle Media, and the money we’re investing is going toward development and content creation. It’s truly being invested in the company.” This acquisition expands Capton’s growing fleet of celebrity-backed companies, which also includes Brad Pitt’s Plan B Entertainment, acquired in 2022, and Peter Chernin’s North Road, added earlier this year.</p><p>Candle Media’s decision to sell off Witherspoon’s firm aligns with its broader strategy to address its substantial debt, which reportedly approximates USD 1 billion. In an attempt to deleverage, the company began divesting original assets, including selling back a digital outlet, which ultimately led to them offloading Hello Sunshine back to its founder at a significantly lower rate than its initial acquisition price.</p><p>Speaking to The Wrap about the buyout, Witherspoon expressed her enthusiasm for the new partnership and the future of her company. “I’m investing alongside Mediawan and joining Pierre-Antoine Capton and his teams because this is the perfect home for this new media landscape. It’s also a homecoming,” she stated. Witherspoon further acknowledged the foundational support, adding, “Peter Chernin and Jesse Jacobs believed in Hello Sunshine from day one. I’m deeply proud of what our incredible team has built and grateful to Blackstone and Candle Media for their partnership and unwavering support. The best is still ahead.”</p><p>What to watch: Future content development from Hello Sunshine under its renewed leadership.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the buyout, matching all key figures and quotes from the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/entertainment/english/hollywood/news/in-2021-a-media-conglomerate-acquired-reese-witherspoons-company-for-usd-900m-years-later-the-actress-and-pierre-antoine-capton-bought-back-100-percent-of-the-firms-shares/articleshow/134990697.cms" rel="noopener">TOI Entertainment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
