     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-16 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Spain Records Hottest September in History With 22.5°C Average Temperature</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Mainland Spain experienced its warmest September since record-keeping began in 1961, with the national weather agency AEMET reporting an average temperature of 22.5°C. This exceptional heat exceeded the 1991–2020 reference period norm by 3.4°C and surpassed the historical average summer temperature of 22.1°C for the first time. The municipality of El Granado matched the all-time monthly heat record by reaching 45.7°C on September 3.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-16/spain-records-hottest-september-in-history-with-225c-average.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>In addition to the unprecedented heat, the month was exceptionally dry, recording only 36% of normal September rainfall. AEMET issued rare red heat alerts in parts of Andalusia during a month made effectively into another summer by human-driven climate change. These extreme temperatures have triggered drought conditions and raised concerns over public health across Europe.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Precisely reports temperature averages, records, rainfall deficit and alerts as per source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-spain-sizzles-hottest-september-weather.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
