     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-10-16 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Study Finds Light Influences Gharial Basking Behavior</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#environment</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A study of captive Gangetic gharials at the Deori EcoCentre in Madhya Pradesh reveals that light acts as a stronger cue for morning basking than temperature. Researchers observed 382 gharials, noting that relying on light rather than heat to initiate basking can lead to suboptimal site selection or hypothermia if artificial light disrupts natural cues.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-16/study-finds-light-influences-gharial-basking-behavior.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Lead author Megha Khanduri warned that light pollution may cause these reptiles to emerge from the water when temperatures are insufficient. Furthermore, if hatchlings respond to light differently than adults, they risk premature exposure to terrestrial predators during early hours, potentially threatening their survival.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate summary of the research findings and potential risks identified in the study.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://india.mongabay.com/2026/10/light-may-influence-morning-basking-behaviour-in-captive-gharials/" rel="noopener">Mongabay India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
