     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-10-16 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Supreme Court Curbs Mid-Course Fee Hikes by Private Professional Colleges</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Supreme Court</span> <span>#Education Fees</span> <span>#Private Colleges</span> <span>#BTech</span></div>
       <p>The Supreme Court has ruled that private professional colleges are largely barred from increasing fees midway through a course, except under exceptional circumstances. This decision offers significant relief to students and families planning for higher education costs, emphasizing the need for clarity on fees for the entire course duration before admission. The court stated that any fee increase after admission should only be permitted in unforeseen situations, such as an unexpected rise in expenditure, according to a TOI report.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-16/supreme-court-curbs-mid-course-fee-hikes-by-private-professi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This ruling came as the court invalidated a 37 percent fee hike imposed on BTech students enrolled in the 2023-27 batch at Dr BC Roy Engineering College in West Bengal. The institution had also introduced a new development fee. A bench comprising Justices PS Narasimha and Alok Aradhe highlighted that financial uncertainty can impose a substantial burden on students and their families, stressing that regulatory authorities must ensure students are aware of their financial commitments upfront.</p><p>Professional education in India often requires considerable financial planning, with many families relying on years of savings or education loans. The court noted that the cost of professional education is significantly higher, referencing reports that indicate fees for professional courses are 711% higher than general courses. Against this backdrop, unexpected fee increases can disrupt financial planning, potentially affecting students' education and the quality of future professionals.</p><p>The bench acknowledged the contribution of self-financing institutions but reiterated the necessity of a predictable fee structure. It clarified that the existing regulatory framework, including principles from previous Supreme Court judgments, AICTE regulations, and the National Education Policy 2020, mandates that relevant authorities notify fees for the entire course before admissions. Fee revisions cannot be introduced arbitrarily post-enrollment, though changes may be allowed in exceptional cases like unforeseen expenditure increases. State governments and regulatory bodies are urged to establish systems protecting students from financial uncertainty.</p><p>The specific case involved students of the 2023-27 BTech batch at Dr BC Roy Engineering College who challenged the 37% fee hike and a new development fee. The Supreme Court sided with the students, quashing the increase and reinforcing the principle that students must be able to assess the full financial cost of a professional degree before committing to a college. A fee structure announced at admission should generally remain constant throughout the course, with post-admission increases justified only by exceptional circumstances, not as routine decisions.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thorough and well-structured report that captures nearly all key details from the source text accurately.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/services/education/sc-new-ruling-on-college-fees-private-engineering-and-medical-colleges-cant-hike-fees-midway/articleshow/134991721.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
